WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Film Courage: How did you get your first&nbsp;
script in the hands of John Carpenter?

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.320
Jim Agnew, Screenwriter/Producer: Well, it was&nbsp;
the first thing I wrote. I’d been working as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.160
a producer on indie films and music videos and&nbsp;
commercials and like I’m going to take the leap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:18.480
into screenwriting. I saved enough money for&nbsp;
one year to just work on screenwriting and had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:25.920
this idea to do a very pulp fiction-type horror&nbsp;
film, kind of non-linear. I had a friend who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.120
a screenwriter (Sean Keller) who had become my&nbsp;
writing partner and we’d never worked together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:39.040
We just decided to write it and after three months&nbsp;
just working on the script with no expectations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.720
We ended up getting a manager from it because&nbsp;
it was so unusual and odd and different than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.240
what was out there. Then from there I get a call&nbsp;
a few weeks later saying John Carpenter read it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.800
loves it! I grew up watching his movies and by&nbsp;
the way he’s the greatest guy on the face of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.520
earth. He’s the coolest dude you could ever&nbsp;
meet. He read the script, liked it and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:05.040
very interested in directing it. His process was&nbsp;
to sit there with the script and read it aloud to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:11.600
us page-by-page and everything that he didn’t like&nbsp;
or thought wasn’t good he’d go Come on, you can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.360
better than that. Which was a learning experience&nbsp;
in itself and it showed, it taught me a lot about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.120
what directors are looking for and what they want&nbsp;
and he’s a great teacher. The film never got made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:28.640
but it was a really good experience and then from&nbsp;
that we actually did an uncredited rewrite on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:35.600
last film THE WARD, so it worked out great because&nbsp;
we got paid to rewrite the film. So we’ve worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.720
with Carpenter twice and he’s a really good dude.
Film Courage: And so this was the second script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.200
with you and your writing partner?
Jim: That was actually the first one.

